yeah battle scenes are actually  very hard to write um i've of course  done quite a few of them in uh the song  of ice and fire books  um very different from marco's battles  of course he's uh he's dealing with uh  warfare on other planets and space with  high-tech weaponry and uh  missiles and and uh  bombs and  projectiles of various sorts and i'm  dealing with big hairy guys with axes  and swords wearing metal and trying to  stick each other with spears or firing  arrows  but some of the basic challenges are  still the same no matter the level of  technology or weaponry  i've read a lot of military history  i've i've always been fascinated by that  and it kind of frustrated by the way  battles are often done in state  television and film in particular  um  the challenge is uh  to try to get both aspects of the battle  but the general's eye and the private  side you know the general's eye  you know the general's sitting back in  the battle and he's he's seeing the  front and he's seeing  okay my left flank is getting pushed  back and i better send in my reserve  there and let me swing the cavalry  around here and here's some important  high ground i better add here so he's  seeing all the all the tactical and  strategic aspects of the battle and he's  playing it out like a  chess  um  game  um and that's fascinating for someone  who likes  military history and the aspects of  warfare that are covered by that uh but  it can tend to be dry now the private  side view is much more visceral it's you  know here i am standing on the battle  and oh my god this big guy is coming at  me with an axe i better get my shield up  oh my god he hit me well wait a minute i  stand him in the belly now his guts are  falling out it's much more visceral it's  much more exciting but you don't have  any sense of the bigger battle so the  real challenge of writing a good battle  scene  is uh  to try to capture both of those in the  scene to try to give the reader a sense  of of the tactics of the battle and  what's happening on a larger battlefront  while still  putting you in the eyes and ears of your  your protagonist and kind of putting  your reader right in this  middle of the action which is what's  going to get the blood pumping and the  excitement going  um next subject war and  violence of course there is a lot of  violence in a book especially in the  santa fe ice and fire but  you treated it very seriously it's never  for fun  it's very relationship  with your life during the vietnam war  the way you treat violence  well you know war has been  a central part of fantasy since tolkien  and even before  i mean you can already go back to the  elite and you know you've got a fantasy  classic set around the trojan war  so  i think  it's certainly a legitimate subject to  write about  but i do think it is a subject that's of  great importance maybe the most  important thing  of all  and  it should not be treated  i don't know for fun um  because it's not i don't know to me at  least it's not funny yeah you can get  humor out of everything i don't want to  let me  rephrase that  you can do funny things about war sure  people have done and who do and you can  find black humor in anything and that's  good too i appreciate black humor  but for me  i worked in network television  for uh american network television for  10 years back in the 80s and 90s  and  being a broadcast station we were  subject to network standards and  practices that is censorship  and they they tried to keep out old sex  was one of the things and the other  thing is they they always tried to tone  down the violence in fact they probably  would have said well we don't want any  violence in our show  but we do want lots of action  and action is what is action the network  might give you a different definition  but to my mind action is bloodless  violence  you know you you see action an action  film here's an exciting car chase and  all the cars are are  slamming against each other and they're  flipping over and they're going into the  ditch  and  the the  flames are engulfing the cars that have  crashed you never actually see the  twisted bodies you never see the person  who when the car slammed into each other  their leg was severed and they're  bleeding to death in the in the front of  that car you never see the drivers  inside the car that's just blown up  screaming in agony as the flesh consumes  them um  that's the sign of action the network  doesn't want to show you because it  might disturb you  well sorry i think you should be  disturbed  i'm  my position on this has really  kind of articulated over the last  decades since i did this and i'm against  action i i mean i'm you can do a  straight character piece with no deaths  in it and and no  no one being in jeopardy and all that  and that's fine you know you can write  my dinner with andre or or um a woody  allen comedy and all that but if you're  going to do something where people are  dying if you're going to do a war story  especially then show war the way it is  and the way it is is complicated because  the truth is we like war why is there  this aptitude for action because like  achilles who had the choice between a  short and glorious life or a long and  boring one he chose a short and glorious  life and he wanted to go out in a blaze  of glory and uh indeed he did  um  the ancients always talked about the  glories of war and you can still see  some of that in the pageantry in the  middle ages which i reflect  in game of thrones and the knights with  their armor their armor shining in the  sun the banners and in a world that was  largely brown and mud colored here were  these incredible warriors with their  heraldry with their eagles and their  lions and their wolves flying from the  banners  it has a beauty to it of frightening  beauty but when then when those armies  clash then you get the severed limbs and  you get the lakes of blood and you get  all of that and i think if you're gonna  honestly treat with that material show  both sides and that's what i try to do  show the glory but also show the pain  and the grief and the cost of the glory  the price of war  i'm not a pacifist  but i was a conscientious objector  during the vietnam war  um  because i thought that particular war  was immoral  and  illegal in fact as most wars by america  have been illegal over the last  well pretty much my lifetime  they would always ask you when you  applied but you know you had to have an  interview and you had to answer  questions when you applied for country  objective status and there were some  draft boards in the united states that  only wanted to correct it to total  pacifists people who at least would  claim why i would never fight under any  circumstances i would never take a human  life i certainly don't  claim that for myself i never have taken  a human life but i think on the right  circumstances i could and they always  say well what would you do if you know  adolf hitler was raping your grandmother  yeah i would kill adolf hitler if he was  raping my grandmother  i might kill him just for being adolf  hitler uh  but you know it was always would you  fight in world war ii yeah yeah muslims  would fight in world war ii um  but world war ii wasn't  the war on the menu at the present time  and one of the things i decided then  grew up grappling with these issues was  that ho chi minh was not a wolf hitler  and in fact he was certainly not sauron  the issue that much of us have to deal  with as fantasy writers it's all very  easy to to always write these books in  which way sauron is always the villain  or more goth even worse or you know and  you're writing you're fighting against  the dark lord or the rotten one or the  bad guy or  you know lord  malice or whatever the hell you're gonna  call your your big bad but  real life doesn't really offer us that  and i wanted to inject a certain level  of realism into  the kind of fiction i was writing  thank you very much

